# A receiver that is proven to be of a few layouts is told apart by a row of tests of the shape, and behind each test the method is known. Is it copied into its arm? Only where the copy
# learns more of `this` than the method does in its own unit. `twice` is inherited: by itself it knows `this` as any Shape, and in the arm of Square its `this.area()` is Square.area: it is
# copied, and nothing is called. `area` is declared by each class: by itself it knows `this` as well as an arm would, so it gets no arms: one test for the classes that have the method, and
# one call through the method table behind it. Such copies were four fifths of
# what the rows cost a build and of their bytes, and a hundredth of what they bought.
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: = ShapeTest {{*}}layouts=[L1]
# CHECK-NOT: = CallDirect
# CHECK: = Mul {{*}}form=Numbers
# CHECK: = ShapeTest {{*}}layouts=[L2]
# RUN(AREAS): walk f2 mechanisms=all
# AREAS-LABEL: code f2:Body
# AREAS: = ShapeTest {{*}}layouts=[L1, L2]
# AREAS: = CallTable
# AREAS-NOT: = ShapeTest
# AREAS-NOT: = CallDirect
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell
# ANSWERS: 75
# ANSWERS-NEXT: 35
